How all these fit together

Imagine you're working at Nokia, Ericsson, Samsung Networks, Rakuten, AT&T, Verizon, Airtel, Jio, etc.

You want to deploy thousands of 5G base stations.

You cannot manually configure them.

So you need:

Kubernetes
      ↓
CRDs
      ↓
Operators
      ↓
GitOps
      ↓
KPT / Porch
      ↓
Automated management of RAN/O-RAN
      ↓
NCOM

1. Kubernetes (K8s)

Foundation layer.

Kubernetes manages:

Pods
Containers
Services
ConfigMaps
Deployments

Think:

AWS EC2 -> manages VMs

Kubernetes -> manages containers


Must learn:

Pods
Deployments
StatefulSets
Services
ConfigMaps
Secrets
Ingress
Helm
2. CRD (Custom Resource Definition)

Kubernetes knows only built-in objects:

Pod
Deployment
Service


But telecom needs objects like:

CellSite
RadioUnit
DU
CU
5GCore


So we create our own resources.

Example:

apiVersion: telecom.com/v1
kind: CellSite
metadata:
  name: bangalore-site-1


This is a CRD.

Think:

CRD = create your own Kubernetes objects

3. Operator

Once CRD exists, who manages it?

Operator.

Example:

kind: CellSite
spec:
  sectorCount: 3


Operator watches:

CellSite created
      ↓
Deploy DU Pods
      ↓
Deploy CU Pods
      ↓
Configure Network


Operator = automation controller.

Formula:

CRD + Controller = Operator


Examples:

Prometheus Operator
Elastic Operator
Nokia Operators
OpenShift Operators
4. GitOps

Most important modern concept.

Traditional:

Engineer
   ↓
kubectl apply
   ↓
Cluster


GitOps:

Git Repo
   ↓
ArgoCD / Flux
   ↓
Cluster


Git becomes source of truth.

Benefits:

Auditability
Rollback
Compliance
Automation

Tools:

ArgoCD
Flux

Must know:

Pull model
Reconciliation
Desired State
5. KPT

Google's configuration management tool.

Think:

Helm = Template Engine

KPT = Configuration Packaging System


Used heavily in telecom.

Example:

5G Cell Site Package
      ↓
Customize
      ↓
Deploy


Capabilities:

Package configs
Version configs
Customize configs

Similar to:

apt-get for Kubernetes configs

6. Porch

Built on top of KPT.

Think:

Git manages code

Porch manages KPT packages


Used in:

Network automation
Site management
O-RAN deployments

Workflow:

Package Repository
       ↓
Porch
       ↓
Customize
       ↓
Approve
       ↓
Deploy

7. RAN (Radio Access Network)

Telecom concept.

Components:

Phone
   ↓
Tower
   ↓
Carrier Network


The tower portion is RAN.

Components:

UE (Phone)
   ↓
RU (Radio Unit)
   ↓
DU (Distributed Unit)
   ↓
CU (Centralized Unit)


RAN handles:

Radio transmission
Scheduling
Mobility
Handover
8. O-RAN (Open RAN)

Traditional model:

Ericsson equipment
      ↓
Ericsson software
      ↓
Ericsson management


Vendor lock-in.

O-RAN says:

RU -> Vendor A
DU -> Vendor B
CU -> Vendor C


Everything communicates through open interfaces.

Benefits:

Open standards
Lower cost
Multi-vendor ecosystem

Important concepts:

RU

Radio Unit

DU

Distributed Unit

CU

Central Unit

Near-RT RIC

Real-time controller

Non-RT RIC

AI/ML optimization

A1 Interface

Policy interface

E2 Interface

Control interface

O1 Interface

Management interface

9. NCOM

NCOM usually refers to a Network Configuration & Operations Management platform (many telecom vendors use similar names, including Nokia products).

Purpose:

Manage thousands of network elements


Provides:

Inventory management
Configuration management
Fault management
Monitoring
Lifecycle management

Think:

Kubernetes = manages containers

NCOM = manages telecom network infrastructure

